blindrsa — provenance note
===========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `adaptor`/
`bip340`/`musig2` modules — self-contained crypto modules not folded into
the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

blindrsa implements **RFC 9474, "RSA Blind Signatures"** (F. Denis,
F. Jacobs, C. A. Wood; IRTF, October 2023), a public IETF specification.
Per CONVENTIONS.md §5's merger-doctrine note, implementing a public spec
is not "derived from" anyone's copyrighted source — no NOTICE entry would
strictly be required for that alone. It is included here anyway because
this module's `pssEncode`/MGF1 helpers duplicate, by design, functionality
the sibling `rsa` module already has privately (see SPEC.md's "Why this
module carries its own pssEncode/MGF1"), and because one third-party
implementation WAS consulted as a design reference (below).

1. **RFC 9474 itself** — the specification implemented. Fetched directly
   from https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9474.txt (the RFC Editor's own
   plain-text rendering) on 2026-07-14, NOT from a summarized,
   paraphrased, or third-party-rendered copy — the Appendix A test
   vectors in `kat_vectors.zig` were extracted from that exact fetch by
   line-range slicing (not free-text search, to avoid accidentally
   matching the RFC's own Table of Contents, which repeats every section
   title including the Appendix A subsection headings) and independently
   cross-checked in Python before being embedded (see `kat_vectors.zig`'s
   own doc comment for the specific algebraic identities checked: `p*q ==
   n`, `e*d == 1 mod lcm(p-1,q-1)`, the Blind/BlindSign/Finalize relations
   over `encoded_msg`/`inv`/`blinded_msg`/`blind_sig`/`sig`, and — for
   `encoded_msg` specifically — an independent from-scratch Python
   re-implementation of EMSA-PSS-ENCODE that reproduces the RFC's
   published `encoded_msg` byte-for-byte for both Appendix A.1 and A.4).
2. **jedisct1/zig-blind-rsa-signatures** (`jedisct1/rust-blind-rsa-signatures`'s
   Zig sibling port, MIT license —
   https://github.com/jedisct1/zig-blind-rsa-signatures). Consulted as a
   DESIGN REFERENCE ONLY (CONVENTIONS.md §5): its public API shape
   (separating a client-held blinding-context type from the raw
   blind/finalize functions, and exposing the RSABSSA variants as
   parameters rather than as separate function names) was read to confirm
   this is a reasonable, idiomatic way to structure a Zig API around RFC
   9474's client/server split. NO source lines were copied or ported —
   this module's actual function names (`blind`/`blindSign`/`finalize`/
   `verify`/`prepareIdentity`/`prepareRandomize`/`pssEncode`), types
   (`Context`), error sets, and every line of Zig code are independently
   written directly from RFC 9474's own algorithm text and RFC 8017's
   EMSA-PSS-ENCODE/MGF1 specifications, not from jedisct1's Rust-derived
   Zig code.
3. **RFC 8017 (PKCS #1 v2.2)** — §9.1.1 (EMSA-PSS-ENCODE) and §B.2.1
   (MGF1), independently re-implemented in `root.zig`'s `pssEncode`/
   `mgf1Xor` because the sibling `rsa` module's own equivalents
   (`emsaPssEncode`/`mgf1Xor`) are private/unexported (see SPEC.md). Same
   public-spec/merger-doctrine basis as item 1 — this is clean-room
   spec transcription, not a copy of `rsa`'s private source, and was
   validated against RFC 9474's own published `encoded_msg` values before
   being trusted (see item 1).

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.
`std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha384`/`std.crypto.ff` (MIT, part of Zig itself)
and the sibling `rsa` module (this repository, MIT — see `../rsa/
README.md`'s "Provenance" section and the root `/NOTICE`) are used via
their public APIs only.

One additional derived value: `kat_vectors.zig`'s `r` (the blinding
factor) is NOT published in RFC 9474 (the RFC publishes only `inv` =
r⁻¹ mod n). It was recomputed as `pow(inv, -1, n)` in Python and
cross-checked both ways (`r·inv ≡ 1 mod n`, and `blinded_msg ≡
encoded_msg · r^e mod n` for both A.1 and A.4) before embedding — it is
arithmetic derived from the RFC's own published values, not third-party
data.

Status
------

**Complete.** All four RFC 9474 §4 operations (`blind` — plus the
deterministic `blindWithFactor` KAT seam — `blindSign` with §7.2
private-op blinding, `finalize`, `verify`), `pssEncode`, and
`prepareIdentity`/`prepareRandomize` are implemented and validated
byte-exact against RFC 9474 Appendix A.1 and A.4 (36/36 tests, Debug and
ReleaseFast). The formerly-missing composite-modulus modular inverse is
implemented locally (`bigModInverse`, extended Euclid over
`std.math.big.int` — independently written from the textbook algorithm,
the same one `rsa`'s private key-derivation helper uses; flagged
`TODO(dry)` for a future consolidation into `rsa`'s public API). See
`SPEC.md`'s "Resolved design decisions" and "Constant-time posture".
